==English==
{{slim-wikipedia}}
[[File:Mortar. - panoramio.jpg|thumb|A mid-19th century naval mortar.]]
[[File:M2-Mortar.jpg|thumb|A mid-20th century infantry mortar.]]
[[Image:Mozdzierz kuchenny.jpg|thumb|right|Mortar and pestle.]]

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|morter||archaic}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|morter}}, from {{der|en|fro|mortier}}, from {{der|en|la|mortārium}}. {{doublet|en|mortarium}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈmɔːtə(ɹ)/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-mortar.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔː(ɹ)tə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A mixture of [[lime]] or [[cement]], [[sand]] and [[water]] used for [[bonding]] [[building block]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1846|author={{w|Nathaniel Hawthorne}}|title={{w|Mosses from an Old Manse}}|chapter=Fire Worship|passage=The holy hearth! If any earthly and material thing, or rather a divine idea embodied in brick and '''mortar''', might be supposed to possess the permanence of moral truth, it was this.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|military|historical}} A short, heavy, large-bore [[cannon]] designed for [[indirect fire]] at very steep [[trajectories]].
# {{lb|en|countable|military}} A relatively lightweight, often portable [[indirect fire]] [[weapon]] which transmits [[recoil]] to a base plate and is designed to lob [[explosive]] [[shell]]s at very steep [[trajectories]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A hollow [[vessel]] used to [[pound]], [[crush]], [[rub]], [[grind]] or [[mix]] [[ingredient]]s with a [[pestle]].
# {{lb|en|countable}} In paper milling, a [[trough]] in which material is [[hammer]]ed.

